{"working":"Start: clef and three sharps. Then two beamed groups of eight 32nds before the first barline. That is only 16 32nds, so the line starts partway through bar 35. These groups are its second half: E5, then the run up from E4 (E4 F#4 G#4 A4 B4 C#5 D#5), then E5 G#4 A4 B4 C#5 D#5 E5 F#5. The first half of bar 35 (E5 F#5 G#5 A5 B5 G#5 A5 F#5, twice) was on the line before.

After the first barline comes bar 36:
- a group of eight 32nds rising from B4 (B5 B4 C#5 D#5 E5 F#5 G#5 A5);
- four high 16ths with ledger lines (B5 E5 G#5 B5);
- the slurred D6–D5 with a natural on the D;
- the D5 quarter and a rest-like stroke.

Then the double barline with repeat dots (:||:). After it is the two-16th pickup D5 C#5, which is bar 37. Then a barline.

Bar 38: grace note with a sharp (C#5), B4 8th, B4 C#5 16ths, D5 D5 8ths, dotted D5 to B5 with a trill sign ('t'), B5 8th, D5 C#5 16ths. Then a barline.

Bar 39: the same figure again: grace, B4, B4 C#5, D5 D5, dotted D5 to B5 with trill, B5. The closing D5 C#5 D5 B4 is written as a small group of four 32nds. Then a barline.

Bar 40: four beamed groups of four 32nds, each a low note then a repeated high note (A4 A5 A5 A5 / B4 B5 B5 B5 / C#5 C#6 C#6 C#6 / D5 D6 D6 D6). The rising ledger lines show the upward sequence. The line ends here, partway through bar 40. The rest (C#–C#6 … to the end of the bar) is on the next line.

Nothing contradicts the edition. Bars 35 and 40 are partial, but the instructions count them as this line's.","right":true,"insights":"Counting notes against the barlines decided both ends. Only 16 32nds come before the first barline, so the line starts in the middle of bar 35. The line ends after four of bar 40's eight groups of four. The :||: repeat sign and the repeated bar 38/39 figure (grace, trill on the dotted D5 to B5) made the middle easy to place. The dense 32nd beams and high ledger-line notes make exact pitches hard to read. Knowing where the previous line ends, or a clearer scan of the noteheads in the first groups, would have confirmed the start without counting."}
